## Issues

- [x] `src/cli/sync/index.ts:87–90` — On the `writeResult.unchanged` branch, `write("Read .aide/config/brain.aide")` is never emitted. The spec's Good Example for idempotency shows "Read .aide/config/brain.aide" as the first stdout line even on a no-change run, and `outcomes.desired[5]` lists "the parsed brain.aide path" as something that should always be logged — not only when a write occurred. The implementation suppresses this line on the no-change path, so a second sync invocation prints only `.mcp.json already in sync — no changes.` with no breadcrumb showing which file was consulted.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[5]` ("Logs every action it takes on stdout — the parsed brain.aide path, … whether the file was unchanged")
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/sync/index.test.ts:111–129` — Test 3b (idempotent re-run) does not assert that "Read .aide/config/brain.aide" appears in `lines` on the no-change path. The only assertion on stdout is `toContain("already in sync")`. This test would continue to pass even if the fix is applied; it does not currently catch the omission.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[5]` (test coverage gap for the "parsed brain.aide path" log on the unchanged path)
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

Both issues are traceable to the same root: the `if (writeResult.unchanged)` early-return at line 87 was written to produce minimal output on no-change, but the spec's intent is to always surface which file was read — the breadcrumb is useful for confirming sync looked at the right project root. The Good Example in the spec makes this visible, but neither the plan.aide (which focused on string-update and schema-collapse) nor the test author pinned it explicitly.

What would have caught this earlier: the plan's step 3 output-format verification (step 1, last bullet — exit-code contract) checked control flow but not output-line coverage across both branches. A test plan item that explicitly enumerates all expected stdout lines for each scenario, keyed to the Good Examples block, would have surfaced the missing "Read" line in the no-change branch before the test was written. This is a recurring pattern: implementations that short-circuit on "no-op" tend to skip logging that still has diagnostic value — the spec should be explicit that the breadcrumb fires unconditionally.
